an  losing a child  um and then  from my point of view as a mother sort  of when you discover this terrifying  reality that your daughter might be  complicit in a murder over text you  don't even know who this person is you  start to think  what did i do who who  who is this person who who is my  daughter how did this happen and there's  a lot of self-blame  but then at the same time trying to  figure out is this person someone who  has limitations as a mother clearly she  tried to get her daughter some help  um but in the relationship between um  michelle and me i always felt like we  decided that they were unable to ever  really make a connection  despite really wanting to you know  really wanting to have those  heart-to-heart conversations really  wanting to  connect and that their  craft that they fell through  sure and that's a really good answer and  that that makes you wonder too when  you're thinking about how you're  approaching michelle here and that  relationship to you as an actress as  kara knowing that this case is so  controversial in that everybody has such  strong opinions about it and it's a very  gray case in my opinion so  how do you keep like are you separating  cara's belief about the case when you're  putting it to michelle or is there some  kind of blend in there maybe maybe  subconsciously  it's interesting you know i think like  most people who will tune into the show  as an audience member or anyone who's  aware of the case i think most people  will come with a preconceived idea or  judgment of who's  what happened and who's responsible and  everything and i think  what drew me to the project and what i  think they do  is done very well is this exploration of  the peeling back of the layers of each  of the characters  um the situation they found themselves  in the the role that technology played  and social media played  and  really trying to find the humanity in  everybody and the tragedy that it is for  everyone involved  um and then fur